-- Renders vs Reality: How to Read an Architectural Render Before You Buy  (en)
with post as (
  insert into public.blog_posts (status, category_id, author_id, faq)
  select 'draft', (select id from public.blog_categories where slug = $v$3d-visualization$v$), (select id from public.blog_authors where slug = 'vari-studio'), $v$[{"q": {"en": "Are architectural renders accurate?"}, "a": {"en": "They are accurate to the model and the decisions behind them. Geometry built from real drawings can be very precise. Lens choice, lighting, planting, context and omitted details are where an image can drift from what you will experience."}}, {"q": {"en": "What does \"artist's impression\" mean on a property advert?"}, "a": {"en": "It signals that the image is illustrative. In at least one major court case it did not protect a developer whose render misrepresented the apartment, so buyers should still treat renders as part of what they were shown."}}, {"q": {"en": "Why do renders make rooms look bigger?"}, "a": {"en": "Usually because of a wide focal length, a low camera, or furniture smaller than standard. Comparing the image with dimensioned floor plans shows the real proportions."}}, {"q": {"en": "Why are the windows lit in daytime renders?"}, "a": {"en": "It is a stylistic convention that makes a building feel inhabited and separates the façade from the sky. In real daylight, most glazing looks dark or reflective from outside."}}, {"q": {"en": "Can I ask a developer for the camera positions of renders?"}, "a": {"en": "Yes. Any studio working from a 3D model can show where each camera stands on the plan, and a developer confident in the images should have no difficulty sharing it."}}]$v$::jsonb
  where not exists (select 1 from public.blog_post_translations where locale = 'en' and slug = $v$renders-vs-reality$v$)
  returning id
)
insert into public.blog_post_translations (post_id, locale, slug, title, excerpt, html, takeaways, seo_title, seo_description, focus_keyword, ready, word_count)
select post.id, v.locale, v.slug, v.title, v.excerpt, v.html, v.takeaways, v.seo_title, v.seo_description, v.focus, false, v.words
from post, (values
  ($v$en$v$, $v$renders-vs-reality$v$, $v$Renders vs Reality: How to Read an Architectural Render Before You Buy$v$, $v$A render is a set of decisions, not a photograph. What lens, light, planting and editing change, and the questions buyers should ask before they sign.$v$, $v$<p>A render is not a photograph of a building that doesn't exist yet. It is an argument for one. Every image a visualization studio delivers is the result of dozens of decisions: where the camera stands, how wide it sees, what hour the sun sits at, which trees have grown, what has been left out. Most of those decisions are legitimate. Some quietly change what you think you are buying.</p>
<p>We make these images for a living, so this is a guide written from the other side of the screen. It is meant for buyers who are being shown a building through pictures, and for developers who want their pictures to hold up when the keys are handed over.</p>
<h2>Every render is an edited view</h2>
<p>A photograph starts from reality and subtracts: it crops, it chooses a moment. A render starts from nothing and adds. Nothing appears in it unless someone put it there: a cloud, a car, a pot plant, the glow behind a window. That is why the useful question is never "is this render accurate?" but "which choices were made here, and do they change the facts I care about?"</p>
<p>The facts that matter are usually few: how big the rooms are, how much light reaches them, what the building looks like from the street on an ordinary day, what surrounds it, and what the finishes actually are. Every technique below touches one of them.</p>
<h2>The lens decides how big a room feels</h2>
<p>Human vision concentrates on a fairly narrow field. Interior renders are often composed far wider, with focal lengths that would count as wide-angle on a real camera, because a wide lens shows more of the room in a single frame. It also stretches the foreground, pushes the back wall away and makes the space read larger than it will feel when you stand in it.</p>
<p>That is not automatically deceptive. A single image has to communicate a whole room, and photographers of real interiors use wide lenses for the same reason. The problem starts when the width is extreme, or when it is combined with furniture that is smaller than standard, a trick that makes floor area look generous.</p>
<p>Camera height matters as much. A camera placed low, at chest height or below, makes ceilings soar. A camera at standing eye level, with vertical lines kept vertical (what renderers call two-point perspective), tells you something close to what you will actually see.</p>
<p>Our own <a href="/en/work/hearthstone-haven">Hearthstone Haven</a> living room is a good example of a deliberately wide, centred composition. The aim there was to show the full height of the vaulted timber roof and the stone chimney in one frame. If you were buying that room, you would want the plan beside the image to read the true depth.</p>
<p><strong>What to ask:</strong> where was the camera, and at what height? A good studio can mark every camera position and field of view on the floor plan in minutes.</p>
<h2>The hour is chosen, and so is the glow</h2>
<p>Look at almost any portfolio, ours included, and you will find buildings at dusk, or under a soft sky, with every window glowing amber. Our <a href="/en/work/black-home">Black Home</a> exterior shows exactly that: an overcast afternoon, and warm light in every pane.</p>
<p>It is a convention for good reasons. Warm windows signal that a house is lived in, and they separate the façade from the sky. But by day, real glazing mostly reads dark or reflective from the outside. Architects interviewed about renderings have pointed out the same thing: in daylight, windows are typically black. So a glowing façade at midday is a mood, not a forecast.</p>
<p>The same goes for interior light. In <a href="/en/work/penthouse-in-dark-mood">Penthouse in Dark Mood</a>, the space is defined almost entirely by designed artificial light: recessed downlights, linear LED strips in the ceiling and along the stair, backlit display niches. That lighting is part of the interior design. Buyers should treat it as a specification and ask whether it is included, because without it the same room looks very different.</p>
<p><strong>What to ask:</strong> which floor, which orientation, which hour? Is the lighting shown included in the price? Is there a daylight view?</p>
<h2>Trees take years to grow</h2>
<p>Landscaping is where renders and reality drift furthest apart, simply because of time. A visualization usually shows planting the way the landscape architect intends it to look once established. On handover day, many of those trees will be young, staked and a fraction of the height.</p>
<p>In our <a href="/en/work/hne-laboratory">HNE Laboratory</a> images for a project in Germany, the timber-clad building sits among mature conifers and autumn-coloured deciduous trees. Whether that is how the site looks on opening day depends on one fact a picture can't tell you: whether those trees already stand on the site or will be planted.</p>
<p><strong>What to ask:</strong> which trees are existing and which are proposed? What size will the new ones be at planting?</p>
<h2>What isn't in the picture</h2>
<p>Renders are tidy by default, because everything in them has to be modelled on purpose. That makes omission the most common way an image flatters, usually without anyone meaning to mislead:</p>
<p>Air-conditioning condensers on balconies and roofs. Sockets, switches, smoke detectors, sprinkler heads and access panels. Downpipes. The neighbouring building that will block part of the view, or the one that will be built next. Overhead cables, the car park, the bins.</p>
<p>None of these is a scandal on its own. Together they explain why so many buyers feel a finished building looks "busier" than the brochure. The professional standard is simple: the things that change a buyer's decision — a view, a neighbour, a dominant piece of equipment — should not disappear.</p>
<h2>Materials are perfect in a render</h2>
<p>Digital stone has no lippage. Timber grain never repeats awkwardly. Grout lines are perfectly even, and every surface is clean. Rendering engines today simulate light on materials with remarkable physical accuracy, but the material itself is whatever the artist assigned. If the brochure shows a book-matched marble and the finishes schedule says "porcelain, marble-effect", both can be honest. The render just isn't the document that decides it.</p>
<p><strong>What to ask:</strong> for the finishes schedule and, if possible, physical samples or a show apartment.</p>
<h2>When a render becomes a promise</h2>
<p>Courts have started to treat renders as more than decoration. In Australia, <em>Ripani v Century Legend</em> concerned a luxury off-the-plan apartment whose marketing render showed a wide, flowing opening between the living area and the terrace. The trial judge found the render misleading under the Australian Consumer Law.</p>
<p>On appeal, the Full Federal Court ordered a new trial on one narrower question, whether the buyers continued to rely on the render. But it agreed that the "artist's impression" style disclaimers did not neutralise it ([2022] FCAFC 191). The lesson travels well beyond Australia: in an off-plan sale, the images stand in for an inspection, and a disclaimer in small print is not a licence to show something that will not be built.</p>
<p>Rules differ by country. In Dubai, for example, property advertising requires a permit from the regulator, and off-plan adverts carry their own disclosures. Check the current requirements with the authority or your agent rather than assuming a render is exempt.</p>
<h2>A short checklist before you sign</h2>
<ul><li>Put the render next to the floor plan and the site plan, and find the camera.</li><li>Ask which unit, floor and orientation the view represents.</li><li>Ask for a daylight view, not only dusk.</li><li>Ask which trees are existing and which are proposed.</li><li>Ask what is not shown: neighbouring plots, rooftop equipment, services.</li><li>Read the finishes schedule, not the image, for materials.</li><li>Keep copies of the images you were shown when you bought.</li></ul>
<h2>What an honest studio does</h2>
<p>Most visualization work is commissioned by developers and architects, so the incentive to flatter is real. The most valuable thing a studio can offer a developer, though, is images that still look right when buyers move in, because those are the images that don't come back as complaints.</p>
<p><mark>⟦VARI — keep only what is true for your process, rewrite in your own words:⟧</mark></p>
<ul><li>We build from the architect's drawings, not from approximations of them.</li><li>We use eye-level cameras for interiors unless the brief calls for something else, and we can document camera positions on the plan.</li><li>We flag when a client asks to remove something material from a view.</li><li>We deliver a daylight version alongside any dusk hero image.</li></ul>
<p>If you are a developer preparing a launch, our guide to <a href="/en/blog/3d-rendering-for-real-estate-developers">selling off-plan with 3D visuals</a> covers the commercial side. When you are ready to brief a project, see how we work with developers on <a href="/en/3d-rendering-services-for-developers">3D rendering for real estate</a>.</p>
<h2>References</h2>
<ul><li>Federal Court of Australia, <em>Century Legend Pty Ltd v Ripani</em> [2022] FCAFC 191 — judgments.fedcourt.gov.au/judgments/Judgments/fca/full/2022/2022fcafc0191</li><li>Maddocks, "Ripani v Century Legend goes to appeal": maddocks.com.au/insights/off-the-plan-sales-misleading-deceptive-conduct</li><li>REIQ, "Developer liable for misleading and deceptive hero render": reiq.com/articles/developer-liable-for-misleading-and-deceptive-hero-render-in-off-the-plan-sale</li><li>The Real Deal, "Architects unpack the pretty pictures" (daylight glazing): therealdeal.com/2015/02/06/riffing-on-renderings-architects-unpack-the-pretty-pictures/</li><li>Bayut agent portal, Trakheesi permit guide (Dubai advertising permits): bayut.com/agentportal/the-real-estate-agents-guide-to-applying-for-a-trakheesi-permit/</li></ul>$v$, $v$["Every render is an edited view: camera, lens, hour, planting and omissions are all choices.", "Wide lenses and low cameras make rooms look larger; compare every render with the dimensioned plan.", "Glowing windows, designed lighting and mature trees are conventions, so ask what the building looks like on an ordinary day.", "Courts have treated off-plan renders as part of what a buyer was shown, and disclaimers do not automatically neutralise them."]$v$::jsonb, $v$Renders vs Reality: How to Read an Off-Plan Render$v$, $v$A render is a set of decisions, not a photograph. A visualization studio explains what lens, light, planting and editing change, and what buyers should ask.$v$, $v$renders vs reality$v$, 1503)
) as v(locale, slug, title, excerpt, html, takeaways, seo_title, seo_description, focus, words);

-- AI Architectural Rendering vs a Professional Studio: What AI Can and Can't Do  (en, de)
with post as (
  insert into public.blog_posts (status, category_id, author_id, faq)
  select 'draft', (select id from public.blog_categories where slug = $v$3d-visualization$v$), (select id from public.blog_authors where slug = 'vari-studio'), $v$[{"q": {"en": "Will AI replace architectural visualization?", "de": "Ersetzt KI die Architekturvisualisierung?"}, "a": {"en": "It is replacing some early-stage and low-stakes image making, and it is becoming part of every studio's toolkit. Work that has to match drawings, stay consistent across views and stand up to scrutiny still relies on 3D production. Expect the work to shift, not disappear.", "de": "Sie ersetzt einen Teil der frühen, unverbindlichen Bilder und wird Bestandteil jedes Studio-Werkzeugkastens. Bilder, die zu den Plänen passen und über mehrere Ansichten konsistent bleiben müssen, entstehen weiterhin als 3D-Visualisierung."}}, {"q": {"en": "Can AI make a render from my floor plan or SketchUp model?", "de": "Kann ich mit ChatGPT eine Architekturvisualisierung erstellen?"}, "a": {"en": "Image-to-image tools can produce attractive images from a screenshot or sketch, and they are useful for exploring ideas. They don't reliably preserve dimensions or keep the design identical across several views.", "de": "Für Ideen und Stimmungen ja. Maßhaltigkeit und gleichbleibende Ansichten desselben Gebäudes liefert ein Bildmodell nicht zuverlässig."}}, {"q": {"en": "Are AI renders accurate enough for real estate marketing?", "de": "Sind KI-Bilder für die Vermarktung von Bauträgerprojekten geeignet?"}, "a": {"en": "Usually not on their own. Buyers compare images with plans and, later, with the finished building. An image that changes the design between views, or misstates a space, creates commercial and legal risk.", "de": "Allein meist nicht. Käufer vergleichen Bilder mit Grundrissen und später mit dem fertigen Gebäude. Abweichungen schaden dem Vertrauen und können rechtliche Fragen aufwerfen."}}, {"q": {"en": "Is AI rendering cheaper than a rendering studio?"}, "a": {"en": "The tools cost far less, but they produce drafts rather than verified deliverables. For decision-grade images, the relevant comparison is the cost of a mistake, not the subscription."}}, {"q": {"en": "Who owns AI-generated renders?", "de": "Sind KI-generierte Visualisierungen urheberrechtlich geschützt?"}, "a": {"en": "It depends on the jurisdiction and on how much human authorship is involved. In the US, purely AI-generated material is not registrable for copyright. Check with a lawyer before relying on AI images in paid marketing.", "de": "Nach deutschem Recht schützt das UrhG persönliche geistige Schöpfungen. Rein KI-generierte Bilder ohne prägenden menschlichen Beitrag gelten nach herrschender Meinung als nicht geschützt. Im Einzelfall rechtlich prüfen lassen."}}]$v$::jsonb
  where not exists (select 1 from public.blog_post_translations where locale = 'en' and slug = $v$ai-architectural-rendering-vs-studio$v$)
  returning id
)
insert into public.blog_post_translations (post_id, locale, slug, title, excerpt, html, takeaways, seo_title, seo_description, focus_keyword, ready, word_count)
select post.id, v.locale, v.slug, v.title, v.excerpt, v.html, v.takeaways, v.seo_title, v.seo_description, v.focus, false, v.words
from post, (values
  ($v$en$v$, $v$ai-architectural-rendering-vs-studio$v$, $v$AI Architectural Rendering vs a Professional Studio: What AI Can and Can't Do$v$, $v$AI image tools changed early design work. Where AI architectural rendering is genuinely useful, where it breaks down, and when a studio still matters.$v$, $v$<p>Type a sentence into an image model and, a few seconds later, you have a glass villa on a cliff at sunset. A few years ago that picture would have taken a visualization artist days. It is no surprise that architects, developers and students now ask the same question in every forum: why pay for rendering at all?</p>
<p>The honest answer is that AI image tools are extraordinary at one half of the job and unreliable at the other. Knowing which half your project is in decides whether AI saves you a week or costs you a launch.</p>
<h2>Two different jobs that both get called "rendering"</h2>
<p>Design moves through two kinds of thinking. Early on it diverges: you want many options, quickly, to discover what a building could be. Later it converges: one design is chosen, and every image must show <em>that</em> design, with that window rhythm, that cladding, that stair, from every angle, without drifting.</p>
<p>Generative AI is built for divergence. A diffusion model produces a plausible image from a prompt or a rough input. Ask again and you get another plausible image. That is a gift when you are exploring, and a liability when you need the façade on Tuesday to match the façade on Monday.</p>
<p>Traditional architectural visualization is built for convergence. The artist constructs a 3D model from drawings, assigns physically based materials, places real-world lighting, and frames cameras. Change the sofa, and only the sofa changes. Move the camera, and the building stays the same building.</p>
<h2>Where AI genuinely earns its place</h2>
<p><strong>Concept and mood.</strong> In the first conversations with a client, AI images are a fast way to agree on a direction: warm minimalism or dark luxury, stone or timber, dusk or noon. Nobody is buying anything from these images, so fidelity matters less than speed.</p>
<p><strong>Sketch-to-image exploration.</strong> Image-to-image tools, many using guidance methods such as ControlNet, can take a hand sketch or a simple massing screenshot and dress it in materials and light. For testing whether a form "works", this is genuinely new, and genuinely useful.</p>
<p><strong>Inside the professional pipeline.</strong> AI is not new to rendering studios. Production renderers such as V-Ray and Corona have shipped AI denoisers for years, cleaning noisy images in a fraction of the render time. AI upscaling and image-editing tools are increasingly part of post-production too. The question has never really been "AI or artists", but which steps machines do best.</p>
<h2>Where it breaks down</h2>
<p><strong>Consistency across views.</strong> Here is a test anyone can run. Take a plan or a simple model of one house and ask an AI tool for three views: front, garden side, and an aerial. Then count the windows, check the roof pitch, and look at where the entrance is. In most attempts, the building quietly changes between images. For a concept board that is fine. For a sales brochure, where buyers compare views, it is a contradiction on paper.</p>
<p><strong>Fidelity to drawings.</strong> A model built from CAD or BIM files carries real dimensions. An AI image carries plausibility. A balcony that is 1.8 metres deep on the drawings may read as three. That is not a cosmetic issue: in off-plan sales, renders are treated as part of what the buyer was shown, and courts have looked hard at images that misrepresented a space (we cover that in <a href="/en/blog/renders-vs-reality">Renders vs Reality</a>).</p>
<p><strong>Specified products and materials.</strong> An interior designer who has specified a particular stone, a named lamp or a fabric needs to see <em>those</em> items. Generative tools invent convincing approximations. A modelled scene uses the actual objects and their real proportions.</p>
<p><strong>Precise revisions.</strong> "Make the joinery 10 cm lower and swap the oak for walnut" is a routine revision in a 3D scene. In a generative workflow it usually means regenerating, and losing details that were already approved.</p>
<p><strong>Animation and walkthroughs.</strong> AI video is advancing quickly, but a walkthrough that holds geometry steady through a camera path, room after room, is still the domain of real 3D scenes and real-time engines.</p>
<p><strong>Ownership.</strong> In the United States, the Copyright Office has said that material generated by AI without sufficient human authorship cannot be registered for copyright. For a developer spending a marketing budget on hero images, that is worth a conversation with a lawyer before the campaign, not after.</p>
<h2>A practical rule for choosing</h2>
<p>Ask one question: <em>will anyone make a decision based on this image being accurate?</em></p>
<p>If the answer is no (an early option, a mood, an internal discussion, a social post that clearly shows an idea), AI is often the faster and cheaper tool, and you should use it.</p>
<p>If the answer is yes (a buyer choosing an apartment, a planning committee, a competition jury, an investor reviewing a scheme, a client approving finishes), you need an image that is built from the design and will survive the comparison with reality.</p>
<p><mark>Many projects need both, in sequence: AI to find the idea, a 3D production to commit to it. AI images also make surprisingly good briefs. They show the mood a client is after far better than adjectives like "warm" or "luxurious" ever do. ⟦VARI: if this matches your experience, say so in your own words, with an example; otherwise keep the general version.⟧</mark></p>
<h2>What this means for cost</h2>
<p>AI tools are typically paid as low monthly subscriptions, while professional visualization is priced per image or per project because each scene is built for one design. The comparison is less "cheap vs expensive" than "draft vs deliverable". For what professional images cost, and what moves the price, see our <a href="/en/blog/architectural-rendering-cost">architectural rendering cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2>How we use it</h2>
<p><mark>⟦VARI — replace with the truth. Possible shapes, keep only what applies:⟧</mark></p>
<ul><li><em>We use AI in early concept stages to explore moods with clients, and we always say when an image is AI-generated.</em></li><li><em>Our production images are built as 3D scenes from the project drawings. AI may assist with denoising or upscaling, never with the design itself.</em></li><li><em>Or:</em> we don't use generative AI in client deliverables at all, and here is why.</li></ul>
<p>Our <a href="/en/work/hne-laboratory">HNE Laboratory</a> images for a project in Germany, and night studies such as <a href="/en/work/light-in-the-dark">Light in the Dark</a>, are the kind of work where every reflection and every façade batten has to belong to one consistent building.</p>
<p>If you have an AI concept you love and need images that match the real design, <a href="/en/contact">tell us about the project</a>.</p>
<h2>References</h2>
<ul><li>U.S. Copyright Office, <em>Copyright Registration Guidance: Works Containing Material Generated by Artificial Intelligence</em> (2023), and <em>Copyright and Artificial Intelligence, Part 2: Copyrightability</em> (2025): copyright.gov/ai</li><li>Chaos, V-Ray documentation, <em>Denoiser</em> (NVIDIA AI and Intel Open Image Denoise options): docs.chaos.com</li><li>CGArchitect, "How AI could change the fabric of architectural visualization": cgarchitect.com/features/articles/5eff6d78-how-ai-could-change-the-fabric-of-architectural-visualization</li><li>The Nation, "No, AI Is Not 'Disrupting' Architecture": thenation.com/article/culture/ai-architecture-midjourney-dall-e</li></ul>$v$, $v$["Generative AI excels at exploring options; 3D production excels at holding one design steady.", "AI images rarely keep a building consistent across several views. Test it yourself with three views of one house.", "If anyone will make a decision based on the image being accurate, use an image built from the drawings.", "AI is already part of professional pipelines, for example denoising and upscaling."]$v$::jsonb, $v$AI Rendering vs Professional Archviz: What AI Can't Do Yet$v$, $v$AI image tools changed early design work. Here's where AI architectural rendering is genuinely useful, where it breaks down, and when a studio still matters.$v$, $v$ai architectural rendering$v$, 1127),
  ($v$de$v$, $v$architekturvisualisierung-ki$v$, $v$Architekturvisualisierung mit KI: Was sie heute leistet – und wo sie scheitert$v$, $v$KI-Bilder sind schnell, aber nicht verbindlich. Wo KI in der Architekturvisualisierung hilft, wo sie scheitert und wann ein Studio unverzichtbar bleibt.$v$, $v$<p>Ein Satz in ChatGPT oder Midjourney, und Sekunden später steht da ein Holzbau im Abendlicht, mit Kiefern im Hintergrund und Menschen auf dem Vorplatz. Vor wenigen Jahren hätte so ein Bild ein Visualisierungsbüro mehrere Tage gekostet. Kein Wunder also, dass in Architekturbüros und bei Bauträgern dieselbe Frage auftaucht: Brauchen wir für Visualisierungen überhaupt noch ein Studio?</p>
<p>Die kurze Antwort: für manche Bilder nicht mehr, für andere mehr denn je. Entscheidend ist, wofür das Bild gebraucht wird.</p>
<h2>Entwerfen ist nicht dasselbe wie Festlegen</h2>
<p>In jedem Projekt gibt es zwei Phasen. Am Anfang sucht man: Varianten, Stimmungen, Materialrichtungen. Später legt man fest: Ein Entwurf ist gewählt, und jedes Bild muss genau diesen Entwurf zeigen, aus jeder Perspektive, mit derselben Fassadenteilung und denselben Fensterachsen.</p>
<p>Generative KI ist ein Werkzeug für die Suche. Ein Bildmodell erzeugt aus einer Beschreibung oder einer Skizze ein plausibles Bild, und beim nächsten Versuch ein anderes plausibles Bild. In der Vorentwurfsphase ist genau das wertvoll. In der Vermarktung ist es ein Problem.</p>
<p>Die klassische Architekturvisualisierung ist ein Werkzeug für das Festlegen. Grundlage ist ein 3D-Modell aus den Plänen, also aus Grundrissen, Ansichten und Schnitten, mit physikalisch korrekten Materialien und echtem Licht. Ändert man das Sofa, ändert sich nur das Sofa. Bewegt man die Kamera, bleibt das Gebäude dasselbe.</p>
<h2>Wo KI wirklich hilft</h2>
<p><strong>Vorentwurf und Atmosphäre.</strong> Im ersten Gespräch mit Bauherrinnen und Bauherren klären KI-Bilder schnell, wohin die Reise geht: helles Holz oder dunkler Klinker, nordisch-reduziert oder warm und wohnlich.</p>
<p><strong>Skizze zu Bild.</strong> Werkzeuge, die eine Handskizze oder einen einfachen Massenmodell-Screenshot mit Material und Licht versehen, eignen sich hervorragend, um eine Form schnell zu prüfen.</p>
<p><strong>Im Produktionsprozess selbst.</strong> KI ist in Visualisierungsbüros nicht neu. Render-Engines wie V-Ray und Corona nutzen seit Jahren KI-gestützte Denoiser, die verrauschte Bilder in einem Bruchteil der Rechenzeit bereinigen. Auch beim Hochskalieren und in der Nachbearbeitung sind KI-Werkzeuge längst Alltag.</p>
<h2>Wo KI an Grenzen stößt</h2>
<p><strong>Gleichbleibende Ansichten.</strong> Ein einfacher Test: Lassen Sie sich von einem KI-Werkzeug drei Ansichten desselben Hauses erzeugen, Straßenseite, Gartenseite und Vogelperspektive. Zählen Sie dann die Fenster, vergleichen Sie die Dachneigung, suchen Sie den Eingang. Meist verändert sich das Gebäude von Bild zu Bild. Für ein Moodboard ist das egal. Für ein Exposé, in dem Käufer die Bilder nebeneinanderlegen, nicht.</p>
<p><strong>Maßstab und Planbezug.</strong> Ein Modell aus CAD- oder BIM-Daten trägt echte Maße. Ein KI-Bild trägt Plausibilität. Ein Balkon mit 1,80 m Tiefe kann im KI-Bild wie drei Meter wirken. Bei Bauträgerprojekten, die vor Baubeginn verkauft werden, sind die Bilder Teil dessen, was dem Käufer gezeigt wurde. Darauf sollte man sich verlassen können.</p>
<p><strong>Wettbewerbe und Genehmigungsverfahren.</strong> Wer an einem Architekturwettbewerb teilnimmt, sollte vorab die Auslobung lesen: Dort ist festgelegt, welche Darstellungen verlangt und zulässig sind. Und Jurys vergleichen Perspektiven mit Plänen. Auch bei Bürgerbeteiligung oder im Gespräch mit dem Bauamt zählt, dass das Bild das zeigt, was gebaut werden soll, nicht das, was ein Bildmodell für plausibel hält.</p>
<p><strong>Gezielte Korrekturen.</strong> „Die Brüstung zehn Zentimeter höher, die Fassadenlattung enger“ ist im 3D-Modell eine Routinekorrektur. Im generativen Workflow bedeutet es meist: neu erzeugen, und bereits abgestimmte Details verlieren.</p>
<p><strong>Urheberrecht.</strong> Nach deutschem Urheberrecht schützt § 2 UrhG nur persönliche geistige Schöpfungen. Rein KI-generierte Bilder ohne prägenden menschlichen Beitrag sind nach herrschender Meinung nicht geschützt. Wer ein Vermarktungsbudget in Bilder investiert, sollte die Rechtelage vorher klären, im Zweifel mit anwaltlicher Beratung.</p>
<h2>Eine einfache Entscheidungsregel</h2>
<p>Fragen Sie sich: <em>Trifft jemand auf Grundlage dieses Bildes eine Entscheidung, bei der es auf Genauigkeit ankommt?</em></p>
<p>Wenn nein, etwa bei einer frühen Variante, einer Stimmung oder einem internen Abstimmungsbild, ist KI oft das schnellere und günstigere Werkzeug.</p>
<p>Wenn ja, etwa bei Käufern, Investoren, einer Jury, dem Gemeinderat oder einer Bauherrschaft, die Materialien freigibt, braucht es ein Bild, das aus dem Entwurf entsteht und den späteren Vergleich mit dem fertigen Gebäude besteht.</p>
<p>Viele Projekte brauchen beides, nacheinander: KI für die Idee, die 3D-Visualisierung für die Festlegung. KI-Bilder sind dabei oft ein gutes Briefing, weil sie die gewünschte Stimmung genauer zeigen als jede Beschreibung.</p>
<h2>Ein Beispiel aus Deutschland</h2>
<p>Für das <a href="/de/work/hne-laboratory">HNE Laboratory</a>, ein Projekt in Deutschland, entstanden Visualisierungen für DGI Bauwerk Gesellschaft von Architekten mbH. Die Bilder zeigen einen dreigeschossigen Bau mit vertikaler Holzlattung, eingebettet in Kiefern und Laubbäume, mit Menschen im Maßstab auf den Wegen. Genau bei solchen Aufgaben, wo jede Latte und jede Fensterachse zum selben Gebäude gehören muss, endet heute die Zuständigkeit der Bild-KI.</p>
<p><mark>⟦VARI: 2–3 Sätze aus eigener Erfahrung zu diesem Projekt (Grundlagen, Abstimmungsrunden, Lichtentscheidung). Nur echte Angaben.⟧</mark></p>
<h2>Und die Kosten?</h2>
<p>KI-Werkzeuge kosten meist ein überschaubares Monatsabo. Professionelle Visualisierungen werden pro Bild oder pro Projekt kalkuliert, weil jede Szene für einen konkreten Entwurf gebaut wird. Der Vergleich lautet also weniger „billig gegen teuer“ als „Skizze gegen verbindliches Bild“. Was Visualisierungen in Deutschland kosten und wovon der Preis abhängt, erklärt unser Beitrag zu den <a href="/de/blog/architekturvisualisierung-kosten">Kosten einer Architekturvisualisierung</a>.</p>
<h2>Wie wir KI einsetzen</h2>
<p><mark>⟦VARI: wahrheitsgemäß beschreiben oder Abschnitt streichen (siehe englische Fassung).⟧</mark></p>
